    <h1 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl lg:text-[28px] font-semibold text-ink tracking-tight mt-0 mb-4 leading-tight text-balance">
        How to Paste an Image Into an Excel Cell or Google Sheets
    </h1>

    <AuthorByline publishDate="2026-09-29" />

    <figure class="my-6 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft">
        <img src="/blog/paste-image-into-excel-google-sheets-cell.png" alt="Screenshot of PasteDownloadApp with a pasted illustration of a blue mug, set to JPEG and ready to download as a file for a spreadsheet cell" width="1600" height="900" class="w-full aspect-[16/9] object-cover" />
    </figure>

    <div class="text-body text-base sm:text-[17px] leading-relaxed space-y-4 [&_h2]:text-ink [&_h2]:text-lg [&_h2]:sm:text-xl [&_h2]:font-semibold [&_h2]:tracking-tight [&_h2]:mt-8 [&_h2]:mb-2 [&_h2]:leading-tight [&_h3]:text-ink [&_h3]:text-base [&_h3]:sm:text-lg [&_h3]:font-semibold [&_h3]:tracking-tight [&_h3]:mt-6 [&_h3]:mb-2 [&_h3]:leading-tight [&_a]:text-link [&_a]:underline [&_a]:underline-offset-2 [&_a]:decoration-link/30 [&_a]:transition-colors [&_a:hover]:decoration-link">

        <p>You copy a product photo, click a cell in your spreadsheet, and press Ctrl+V. The picture appears, but it is floating on top of the grid rather than sitting in the cell you clicked. Sort the column and the picture stays where it was while the rows move underneath it. Filter the sheet and it hangs over the wrong row. This is the default in both Excel and Google Sheets, and it is the reason "paste image into cell" is such a common search.</p>

        <p>Both apps can now put a picture <em>inside</em> a cell, where it behaves like any other value: it moves when you sort, hides when you filter, and resizes with the row. The option just is not what Ctrl+V does. This guide covers the exact menu paths in current Excel and Google Sheets, how to convert a picture you already pasted, what to do in older versions of Excel that lack the feature, and how to handle a screenshot that is only on your clipboard when the menu insists on a file.</p>

        <h2>Floating Pictures vs In-Cell Pictures</h2>

        <p>It helps to know which of the two you have, because the fix depends on it.</p>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1.5">
            <li><strong>A floating picture</strong> sits on a layer above the grid. You can drag it anywhere, resize it freely, and it can cover several cells. Selecting it shows sizing handles and, in Excel, a Picture Format tab on the ribbon. Sorting, filtering, and formulas cannot see it, because it is not in any cell.</li>
            <li><strong>An in-cell picture</strong> is the cell's value, the same way a number or a piece of text would be. It fits inside the cell's borders, it has no sizing handles, and clicking it selects the cell. It travels with its row when you sort and disappears when a filter hides that row. In Excel, a lookup formula can even return it.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>If you are building a product catalog, an inventory list, a staff directory, or anything else where each row needs its own picture, you want in-cell pictures. Floating pictures are still the right choice for a logo in a report header or an annotated screenshot laid over a chart.</p>

        <h2>Paste an Image Into an Excel Cell (Microsoft 365 and Excel 2024)</h2>

        <p>Current versions of Excel have a dedicated in-cell paste. According to <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/excel/insert-picture-in-cell-in-excel" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Microsoft's own documentation</a>, the Place in Cell feature is available in Microsoft 365 and Excel 2024. With an image on your clipboard:</p>

        <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1.5">
            <li>Click the cell where the picture should go.</li>
            <li>On the <strong>Home</strong> tab, click the arrow under <strong>Paste</strong> and choose <strong>Paste Picture in Cell</strong>. The same option is in the right-click menu under the paste options.</li>
            <li>The image appears inside the cell, scaled to fit while keeping its proportions. Make the row taller and the column wider if you want it bigger.</li>
        </ol>

        <p>If the image is a file on your computer rather than something on your clipboard, go to <strong>Insert</strong>, then <strong>Pictures</strong>, then <strong>Place in Cell</strong>, and pick <strong>This Device</strong>. The same menu also offers stock images and online pictures. Excel accepts JPG, PNG, BMP, ICO, WebP, TIFF, and non-animated GIF files for in-cell pictures.</p>

        <VideoLink
            videoId="9x-6O6FBYPE"
            title="Excel: The NEW and EASIEST Way to Insert Images into Cells"
            note="A walkthrough of Excel's Place in Cell feature, including converting a floating picture into an in-cell picture and how the image resizes with the cell."
        />

        <h3>Converting a Picture You Already Pasted</h3>

        <p>If you pressed Ctrl+V out of habit and now have a floating picture, you do not need to delete it and start over. Right-click the picture and choose <strong>Place in Cell</strong>. Excel moves it into the cell under the picture's top-left corner. To go the other way, right-click a cell that contains a picture and choose <strong>Place over Cells</strong>, which turns it back into a floating picture you can drag and resize.</p>

        <h3>Pictures From a Web Address: the IMAGE Function</h3>

        <p>If your images already live online, for example product photos on your store's CDN, you do not need to paste anything. Microsoft 365 includes an <code>IMAGE</code> function that loads a picture into a cell from a URL:</p>

        <p><code>=IMAGE("https://example.com/photo.jpg", "Blue mug, front view")</code></p>

        <p>The first argument is the address, which must use https. The second is optional alt text for screen readers. A third, optional sizing argument controls the fit: 0 fits the picture inside the cell and keeps its proportions, 1 stretches it to fill the cell, 2 shows it at original size, and 3 lets you give a custom height and width. Because it is a formula, you can fill it down a column and point each row at a different URL stored in another cell.</p>

        <h2>Older Excel Versions: Make a Floating Picture Behave</h2>

        <p>Excel 2021, 2019, and earlier do not have Place in Cell or the IMAGE function. Pictures in those versions are always floating. You can still get most of the behavior you want with one setting:</p>

        <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1.5">
            <li>Paste the picture, then resize it so it fits within the cell's borders. Hold Alt while dragging to snap its edges to the gridlines.</li>
            <li>Right-click the picture and choose <strong>Size and Properties</strong> (on some versions, <strong>Format Picture</strong>, then the Size and Properties icon).</li>
            <li>Under <strong>Properties</strong>, choose <strong>Move and size with cells</strong>.</li>
        </ol>

        <p>Now the picture moves when rows move, shrinks when you shrink the row, and is hidden along with its row by a filter. Sorting also carries it along, as long as the picture sits fully inside a single cell. A picture that overlaps a cell border can end up attached to the wrong row, which is the most common reason this approach seems to fail.</p>

        <h2>Paste an Image Into a Google Sheets Cell</h2>

        <p>Google Sheets works the same way in principle: Ctrl+V gives you a floating picture, and there is a separate command to put one inside a cell.</p>

        <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1.5">
            <li>Click the cell where the picture should go.</li>
            <li>Open <strong>Insert</strong>, then <strong>Image</strong>, then <strong>Insert image in cell</strong>.</li>
            <li>Choose where the picture comes from: an upload from your computer, a web address, your camera, Google Drive, Google Photos, or a Google Images search.</li>
        </ol>

        <p>To convert a picture you already pasted with Ctrl+V, click it, open the three-dot menu that appears at its top-right corner, and choose <strong>Put image in selected cell</strong>. Sheets moves it into whichever cell is currently selected, so click the right cell first, then the picture.</p>

        <p>One rule catches people out: a Google Sheets cell holds either a picture or text, not both. If you want a caption or a product name, put it in the next column.</p>

        <h3>The IMAGE Function in Google Sheets</h3>

        <p>Sheets has had an <code>IMAGE</code> function for years, and it works much like Excel's. <code>=IMAGE("https://example.com/photo.jpg")</code> loads the picture into the cell. An optional second argument sets the mode: 1 fits the picture inside the cell and keeps its proportions (the default), 2 stretches it to fill the cell, 3 shows it at its original size, and 4 lets you give a custom height and width in pixels as the third and fourth arguments. The URL has to be publicly reachable. A link to a private Google Drive file or a page that requires you to be logged in will show an empty cell.</p>

        <VideoLink
            videoId="A2ZOX5bt08o"
