            note="Compares the three ways to get a picture into Sheets: the IMAGE formula, an image inside a cell, and an image floating over cells. Handy for deciding which one fits your sheet."
        />

        <h2>When the Image Is Only on Your Clipboard</h2>

        <p>Excel's Paste Picture in Cell reads straight from the clipboard, so a screenshot you just copied works directly in current versions. Google Sheets is the one that tends to cause trouble. The Insert image in cell dialog is built around uploading a file, and whether it accepts a pasted image depends on your browser and how the image got onto the clipboard. The same is true of Excel for the web in some browsers, and of older desktop versions where you want to use the Insert menu instead of pasting.</p>

        <p>The fix is to turn the clipboard image into a file first:</p>

        <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1.5">
            <li>Open <a href="https://pastedownloadapp.com/">PasteDownloadApp</a> in another tab.</li>
            <li>Press Ctrl+V (Cmd+V on a Mac). The image appears in the preview with its dimensions and size.</li>
            <li>Choose PNG for screenshots with text, or JPEG for photos, and download. The file lands in your Downloads folder with a timestamped name.</li>
            <li>Back in your spreadsheet, use the upload or This Device option and pick the file you just saved.</li>
        </ol>

        <p>The conversion happens inside your browser, so a screenshot of internal data or a customer record is never uploaded anywhere except the spreadsheet you chose. If you are not sure why a copied image is not a file yet, <a href="/blog/where-do-clipboard-images-go/">where screenshots and copied images actually go</a> explains the difference.</p>

        <h2>Getting the Size Right</h2>

        <p>An in-cell picture is only as large as its cell. A default Excel row is about 20 pixels tall, so a pasted photo looks like a thumbnail until you change the row height. A few habits make image-heavy sheets much easier to work with:</p>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1.5">
            <li><strong>Set the row height before you insert.</strong> Select all the rows that will hold pictures and set one height for all of them, for example 80 or 100 pixels. Every picture then scales to the same size.</li>
            <li><strong>Crop before you paste.</strong> The cell shows the whole image, scaled down. If only part of a screenshot matters, crop it first, so the important part is not a few pixels wide inside the cell.</li>
            <li><strong>Do not paste huge originals.</strong> A 4000-pixel phone photo in each of 500 rows makes a workbook slow to open, scroll, and share. The cell will never show that detail anyway. Pictures of roughly 400 to 800 pixels on the long side are plenty for a catalog.</li>
            <li><strong>Pick the format on purpose.</strong> JPEG or WebP keeps photos small. PNG keeps screenshots and logos sharp. The <a href="/blog/png-vs-jpg-vs-webp/">PNG vs JPG vs WebP comparison</a> has real file sizes for both kinds of image.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>

        <h3>I do not see Paste Picture in Cell or Place in Cell</h3>
        <p>Your version of Excel does not have the feature. It is limited to Microsoft 365 and Excel 2024. Check <strong>File</strong>, then <strong>Account</strong>, to see which version you have, and install pending updates if you have a Microsoft 365 subscription. On older versions, use the Move and size with cells approach described above.</p>

        <h3>The picture pasted, but it is tiny</h3>
        <p>That is the cell size, not the picture. Increase the row height and column width. In-cell pictures always scale to fit their cell.</p>

        <h3>Pasting does nothing at all</h3>
        <p>Check that the clipboard actually holds image data. Copying an image's address, or copying a file in File Explorer or Finder, puts text or a file reference on the clipboard, not the picture itself. Right-click the image and use "Copy image", or take the screenshot again with a copy-to-clipboard shortcut. <a href="/blog/access-clipboard-not-working/">Why clipboard access fails</a> covers the other common causes.</p>

        <h3>The IMAGE formula shows an error or an empty cell</h3>
        <p>The URL has to point directly at an image file and be publicly accessible over https. Links to web pages that contain an image, private cloud files, or addresses that require a login will not load. Open the URL in a private browser window. If you see just the image, the formula should work.</p>

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <h3>Can I sort and filter rows that contain pictures?</h3>
        <p>Yes, if the pictures are in-cell pictures. They behave like any other cell value and move with their rows. Floating pictures only follow along in Excel if they are set to Move and size with cells and sit fully inside one cell.</p>

        <h3>Can I put text and a picture in the same cell?</h3>
        <p>Not in Google Sheets, where a cell holds one or the other. In Excel, an in-cell picture is the cell's value, so the practical answer is also no. Use a neighboring cell for the label.</p>

        <h3>Does this work in Excel on a Mac?</h3>
        <p>Yes. Microsoft 365 for Mac includes Place in Cell and the IMAGE function. The paste command is under the same Paste menu, and Cmd+V still creates a floating picture by default.</p>

        <h3>Will the pictures still be there if I download the Google Sheet as an Excel file?</h3>
        <p>Pictures inserted with Insert image in cell are generally carried over when you download a Sheet as .xlsx, but results depend on which version of Excel opens the file. Check the downloaded copy before you delete the original.</p>

